{
  "case_id": "2006-05-85",
  "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Frederick, are you going to get to the 1447(d) problem?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The district court thought it was and one we did, the district court said I have no authority in -- under -- over any of these people."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: One technical question.\nThe same one. I would to be -- imagine it is a diversity case. Imagine that a Federal court erroneously remands it. A trial gets up to this Court.\nAnd we say it was diverse. It shouldn't have been remanded. Now what's -- is that like -- can that happen? I guess. And is the remedy then wipe out the State proceedings, go back to Federal court? What happens? Has that have ever been a case like that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Counsel, what -- what do you do about 1447(e) which says that if after removal joinder might defeat subject matter jurisdiction, the court can deny joinder or permit joinder and remand the action. In other words, you have got subject matter jurisdiction but the statute contemplates it may -- later -- later you may lose subject matter jurisdiction and you can remand it there."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: How is that relevant here?\nWhere is the post-removal event?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Can, can you tell me, if the Petitioner does not prevail here, the case goes back to the State court. Can the immunity argument be raised in the State court?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0001-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF LEONARD B. SIMON, ON BEHALF OF RESPONDENTS\nMR. SIMON: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court.\nThere are two reasons, independent and sufficient reasons why there is in appellate jurisdiction in this case. The first most narrow and simple reason to vacate the decision below for lack of jurisdiction, is to follow this Court's precedents which accept the district court's remand order for what it is and what it says it is, a remand for lack of subject matter jurisdiction under 1447(c), unremovable under 1447(d). That is a simple direct route to that result."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0001-0010",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SIMON: I think B.C. Hydro could have taken a collateral order, a collateral order appeal.\nJUSTICE ALITO: Whether it's a collateral order or not just goes to whether it falls under 1291. It doesn't speak at all to the issue of 1447."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: But I'm having, I'm having the same problem as Justice Scalia and Justice Breyer. Can you say in just a few sentences the difference between the case where there is sovereign immunity, China, the China hypothetical, and this case where it's organ immunity?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Simon",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SIMON: I think there are two differences, so if I could. One is textual and one is important. Let me start with the textual difference. The textual difference is that 1447(d) speaks to remands, and so to the extent the district court remanded the matter to State court the remand is untouchable on appeal or by mandamus because of 1447(d)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0002-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The textual difference is that 1447(d) speaks to remands, and so to the extent the district court remanded the matter to State court the remand is untouchable on appeal or by mandamus because of 1447(d)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: I don't care. I don't call this an appeal of a remand. What this is, it is an Appeal of the determination precedent to remand that Powerex is not an organ."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Simon",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SIMON: Immunity is dispositive and Federal bench trial is not -- and is no different from the day to day decisions district courts make all the time about the choice between a State court and a Federal court in the area of remands and removals."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Frederick",
      "case_id": "2006-05-85",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "39b97a3c45c8e56a946057f649a64c41a4e9d25e9b433c243e613d0e8016355b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-39b97a3c45c8-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FREDERICK: Mr. Chief Justice, a non-jury trial is an aspect of sovereign immunity. This Court's cases have recognized that. In footnote 6 of the Government's brief, the Government cites the Lehman case which makes this point clear and argues that the point should be applicable to sovereigns in the context in which we are faced here."
    },
    {
